Everything is recompiled by running
make clean
make 

cholesky.h declares the pp::cholesky struct.
cholesky.cc implements the decomposition, solve, determinant and inverse functions.
main.cc contains the tests that can be selected from the command line.
linalg.h contains the pp::matrix and pp::vector classes from earlier homeworks.

decomp_check.txt is generated by ./main decomposition
linear_check.txt is generated by ./main linear
determinant_check.txt is generated by ./main determinant
inverse_check.txt is generated by ./main inverse


The Cholesky decomposition requires the matrix to be real, symmetric and positive-definite. 
The matrix has to be a square matrix.

The Cholesky decomposition looks like this: A = LL^T
L is a lower triangular matrix and L^T is the transpose of L and is upper triangular. 

The Cholesky decomposition requires roughly 1/3n^3 arithmetic operations where QR (from the homework) uses 2n^3. So Cholesky is faster. 

I am using the linear algebra stuff from previous homeworks in the linalg.h file and creating new files for the cholesky decomposition to keep the homeworks and examination project separate. 

I will put the cholesky decomposition inside the same namespace (namespace pp) as the QR decomposition and the other linear algebra stuff

To make sure the matrix follows all the requirements I want the constructor to test the following:
1. Check that A is square.
2. Check that A is symmetric.
3. Perform the decomposition.
4. During decomposition, check that each square-root argument is positive.

(it does not need to check if the matrix is real, because my pp::matrix stores every entry as double, which is a real-valued type.)

The algorithm used is the cholesky-banachiewicz algorithm which computes L row by row starting from the upper-left corner.

I use the wikipedia code and help from chatgpt. 

Wikipedia code:
for (i = 0; i < dimensionSize; i++) {
    for (j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        float sum = 0;
        for (k = 0; k < j; k++)
            sum += L[i][k] * L[j][k];

        if (i == j)
            L[i][j] = sqrt(A[i][i] - sum);
        else
            L[i][j] = (1.0 / L[j][j] * (A[i][j] - sum));
    }
}

I split the assignment into separate tests in main.cc:
one for the decomposition, one for the linear equation solver,
one for the determinant, and one for the inverse matrix.
This is done by adding two different tests to main.cc using 
void and then running them separately from inside the main 
function. 

The first test is going to test the implementation of the Cholesky decomposition. I will do this in basically the same way as the homework for linear equations for QR decomposition, just for Cholesky decomposition instead. 

1. Generate a random nxn matrix B (B is not necessarily symmetric nor positive-definite but we use B to create a symmetric positive-definite matrix A in the next step)
2. Construct a symmetric positive-definite matrix A by doing the following A = B^TB + λI where λ is positive.
3. Factorize it into LL^T
4. Check that L is lower triangular
5. Check that the diagonal elements of L are positive 
6. Check that LL^T = A

The matrix B^TB is symmetric because if we transpose it we get (B^TB)^T = B^TB

(AB)^T = B^TA^T transpose rule can be found at: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpose

The matrix B^TB is positive semi-definite.
This is because a matrix is positive semi-definite if
x^TAx >= 0.  (rule found at https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Definite_matrix)
Inserting B^TB gives 
x^TB^TBx = (Bx)^T(Bx) = ||Bx||^2 >= 0

Adding λI guarantees x^TAx = ||Bx||^2 + λ||x||^2 > 0
for every nonzero x. Therefore, A is positive-definite.

The test for this code is run by running
make 
./main decomposition


The next part of the project is to implement the linear equation solver, calculation of determinant and the calculation of the inverse matrix. 

I am making these implementations in a similar way as for the QR decomposition. 

To solve linear equations of the form Ax = b, we insert the decomposition
LL^Tx = b

Introduce an intermediate vector y = L^Tx, so that
Ly = b

Ly = b is solved using forward-substitution. 
The equation Ly = b gives 
L(0, 0)y0 = b0
L(1, 0)y0 + L(1, 1)y1 = b1
L(2, 0)y0 + L(2, 1)y1 + L(2,2)y2 = b2
and so on depending on the size of the matrix.
The first equation only contains y0, the second contains y0 and y1 so we solve from top to bottom. 
The code explanation is written as comments in the code. 

L^Tx = y is solved using back-substitution. 
The equation L^Tx = y gives for a 3x3 matrix
L(0, 0)x0 + L(1, 0)x1 + L(2, 0)x2 = y0
L(1, 1)x1 + L(2, 1)x2 = y1
L(2, 2)x2 = y2
which is the opposite from before so now we need to solve
from the bottom. 

Now for the determinant
det(A) = det(LL^T)
from wikipedia we have
det(LL^T) = det(L)det(L^T)    (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinant)
we also have 
det(L) = det(L^T)
so we can write
det(A) = det(L)det(L^T) = det(L)^2
Since L is a triangular matrix the determinant of L is simply
the product of the entries of its diagonal. 
so det(A) = det(L)det(L) = (product of diagonal entries)^2.

Now we need to calculate the inverse matrix A^-1.
It needs to satisfy AA^-1 = I.
If we name the columns of A^-1 z_j then we need to solve
Az_j = e_j
where e_i are the columns of the identity matrix aka
the ith unit vector. 
This is a linear equation so we can use our linear 
equation solver. 

For testing each of these (linear equation solver, 
determinant solver and inverse matrix solver) there is
a separate test in main.cc

./main linear
./main determinant
./main inverse

The test for the linear equation solver does this:
1. Generate a random square matrix B of modest size. 
2. Construct a symmetric positive-definite matrix A = B^T B + λI, where λ is positive.
3. Generate a random vector b of the same size.
4. Factorize A into A = LL^T
5. Solve LL^Tx = b
6. Check that Ax = b

The determinant is tested on a 2x2 matrix with known 
determinant 8.

The inverse is tested by calculating A^-1 and checking 
that A*A^-1 is approximately the identity matrix.

The tests print true if the checks pass. Very small 
numerical differences from zero are expected because 
of floating-point rounding.